<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mamluk Sultanate</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Mamluk Sultanate was a state ruled by mamluk soldier elites in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> from 1250 to 1517. It replaced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid Empire</a> and defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ain-jalut">Ain Jalut</a> in 1260, halting their advance. It was conquered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> in 1516 to 1517.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mamluk-sultanate.jpg" alt="Mamluk Sultanate" width="320" height="302" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ro4444 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mamluk_Sultanate_of_Cairo_1317_AD.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Even after the Mongol destruction of Baghdad in 1258, the shadow caliphate maintained by the <b>Mamluks</b> in Cairo (1261–1517) served as an important legitimising institution for Muslim rulers across North Africa and the Levant.</blockquote><a href="/empire/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The dynasty's greatest unintended legacy was the <b>Mamluk Sultanate</b> that replaced it: the slave-soldier system on which the later Ayyubids relied produced an institution of exceptional military effectiveness that defeated the Mongols at Ain Jalut in 1260, the first decisive check to Mongol expansion in the Middle East,…</blockquote><a href="/empire/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>From the accession of Barquq in 1382 until 1517, most sultans of <b>Mamluk Egypt</b> and Syria were Circassians who had been brought to Cairo as slave soldiers.</blockquote><a href="/empire/circassia">Circassia · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Enslaved Kipchak youths were sold to <b>Mamluk Egypt</b>, whose rulers became allies of the khans.</blockquote><a href="/empire/golden-horde">Golden Horde · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Trade flourished along the Silk Road, linking China, Central Asia and the Mediterranean, and the Ilkhanate also benefited from diplomatic and commercial contacts with European powers seeking allies against the <b>Mamluks</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Divided by baronial quarrels and by the War of Saint Sabas (1256–1258) between Venice and Genoa, the kingdom faced the <b>Mamluk Sultanate</b>, which defeated the Mongols at Ain Jalut in 1260.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-jerusalem">Kingdom of Jerusalem · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
